= Fenugreek

_Status: classical in_ all four canonical traditions _and substantially the same drug in each — Ayurvedic_ methī / methikā _(Charaka), Tibb_ ḥulba _(Avicenna), Chinese 葫蘆巴 húlúbā (Jiayou Bencao 1057), Greco-Roman_ tēlis / foenum-graecum _(Hippocrates, Dioscorides, Pliny). The Latin species epithet_ foenum-graecum _literally "Greek hay" — one of the cleanest cross-cultural namings in the materia medica, reflecting the Roman import of the herb from Greek-speaking lands as fodder before its medicinal status was elaborated. Modern register: extensively documented as a hypoglycemic, lipid-lowering, and galactagogue drug. Also a_ Rosh Hashanah siman _in the rabbinic-Jewish tradition (Talmudic_ rūbia/rūbya _= fenugreek, BT Horayot 12a / Keritot 6a) and a_ Tibb al-Nabawī _attestation via the hadith "were my Ummah to know what is in fenugreek..." (chain of authenticity contested — flagged)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Trigonella foenum-graecum_ L., family Fabaceae (Leguminosae); the drug is the dried *ripe seed* (_Foenugraeci semen_ in pharmacognosy), with the *fresh and dried leaves* having a distinct culinary-medicinal register (the South Asian _methī sāg_). The genus name _Trigonella_ from Greek τριγωνική (_trigōnikē_, "three-cornered, triangular") — for the triangular shape of the small papilionaceous flower. The species name *foenum-graecum* — "Greek hay" — is the canonical Latin compound: _foenum_ "hay" + _graecum_ "Greek." The Romans imported the plant from Greek-speaking eastern Mediterranean lands as cattle fodder; the medicinal-and-culinary register developed subsequently. The "Greek hay" naming is preserved in the English _fenugreek_, the French _fenugrec_, the German _Bockshornklee_ (a different metaphor — "goat-horn clover" — for the curved seed-pods), and many other European languages.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short (s.v. _foenum-graecum_, _foenum_); de Vaan (s.v. _faenum_); Beekes (s.v. τρίγωνος). The "Greek hay" naming is one of the very few classical plant names that explicitly preserves the importing-culture's identification of the plant's origin: cf. _Punica granatum_ (Punic apple = pomegranate) for a similar Roman naming pattern.]

*English:* _Fenugreek_, from Middle English _fenugreke_, from Old French _fenugrec_, from Latin _foenum-graecum_. The English form preserves the Latin transparently. Older English: _Greek hay_ (now obsolete except in some herbal-historical contexts).#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "fenugreek, n.", "Greek hay, n."). The Modern English form has been stable since the 15th century.]

*Chinese:* *葫蘆巴 (húlúbā)* — the canonical Chinese drug name; a *transliteration of the Persian / Arabic ḥulba* (حُلْبَة) from the Persian medical-pharmacological transmission. The herb is *not in the classical Han-Tang Bencao tradition* — it appears first in the *_Jiayou Bencao_* (嘉祐本草, 1057, Song dynasty), reflecting the Persian-Arabic pharmaceutical transmission of the early Song period; from there into the _Bencao Gangmu_ and the standard Chinese pharmacy. *葫蘆巴 is a Song-era Persian introduction*, distinct from the indigenous Chinese pharmacopoeia of the _Shennong Bencao Jing_. The transliteration register (rather than a descriptive Chinese name) is a clear marker of foreign provenance.#footnote[葫蘆巴 in _Jiayou Bencao_ (嘉祐本草, 1057), then in _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578. The Persian / Arabic transmission to Song China is well-documented; the Song-era pharmaceutical literature absorbed multiple aromatic and medicinal drugs from the Arab-Persian world via maritime and Silk-Road trade.]

*Sanskrit:* *मेथी (methī)* / *मेथिका (methikā)* — the canonical Sanskrit drug names, treated by Caraka, Suśruta, and Bhāvaprakāśa. Other classical Sanskrit names: *चन्द्रिका (candrikā)* — "moon-like" (for the seed shape in some readings); *दीपनी (dīpanī)* — "the kindler" (for the digestive-stimulant action); *जन्तुघ्नी (jantughnī)* — "destroyer of worms" (anthelmintic action); *बहुपत्रिका (bahupatrikā)* — "many-leaved." Modern Hindi *मेथी (methī)* descends directly from the Sanskrit; the Urdu میتھی (_methī_) is a borrowing. The herb is treated as both a *seed-drug* (मेथी बीज, methī bīja) and a *leaf-drug / vegetable* (मेथी पत्ता / मेथी साग, methī patta / methī sāg) — both forms are classical.#footnote[Monier-Williams (s.v. _methī_, _methikā_, _candrikā_, _dīpanī_); Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_śāka-varga_ — for the leaves) and elsewhere; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_ (seed) and _Śāka-varga_ (leaf); P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb register):* *حُلْبَة (ḥulba)* — the canonical Arabic / Tibb name; treated by Avicenna in _al-Qānūn_ Book II as a major drug. Persian: *حلبه / شنبلیله (ḥalbah / shanbalīleh)*; Urdu / Hakeemi: *میتھی (methī)* — borrowed from Sanskrit / Hindi — alongside *حلبه (ḥulbah)*. The Arabic name is the source of the Chinese 葫蘆巴 transliteration, marking the Persian / Arabic transmission route into Song China.#footnote[Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II, _ḥulba_ entry; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _ḥulba_ entry; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_, vol. III, _ḥulba_ entry.]

*Hebrew / Aramaic:* *חִלְבָּה (chilbah)* — cognate with Arabic _ḥulba_; the herb is not directly named in the Hebrew Bible, but the Talmudic-Aramaic *רוּבְיָא (rūbya / rubia)* is identified with fenugreek by the canonical rabbinic tradition. *_Rūbya_ is one of the recognized "simanim" (symbolic foods) of Rosh Hashanah*, the Jewish New Year, eaten with the formulaic prayer *"yehī rāṣōn... she-yirbū zechūyōtēnū"* — "may it be Your will... that our merits increase" — punning on the Aramaic _rūbya_ ("fenugreek") and the Hebrew root _r-b-h_ ("to increase, multiply"). The custom appears in *Babylonian Talmud, Horayot 12a and Keritot 6a* (the foundational text on Rosh Hashanah simanim) and is preserved continuously through the medieval and modern rabbinic traditions.#footnote[Babylonian Talmud, Horayot 12a and Keritot 6a — the simanim of Rosh Hashanah, including _rūbya_ / fenugreek with the _yirbū_ prayer. The identification of Talmudic _rūbya_ with fenugreek is the standard rabbinic interpretation (Rashi, Tosafot, Maimonides) and is preserved in the Sefardic and many Mizrachi-Jewish Rosh Hashanah liturgies; the Ashkenazi rite uses different simanim. Steinsaltz, _Reference Guide to the Talmud_; Y. T. Lewinski, _Sefer Ha-Mo'adim_ — for the customary register.]

*Modern global culinary names:* the herb is one of the most-named plants in the materia medica — _methī_ (South Asia), _ḥulba_ / _shanbalīleh_ (Middle East / Iran), _hilbeh_ (Levantine Arabic — also the name of the Yemeni Jewish fenugreek-and-spice paste eaten at the Sabbath table), _fenugrec_ (French), _Bockshornklee_ (German — "goat-horn clover"), _alholva_ (Spanish — itself a Spanish borrowing from Arabic _al-ḥulba_, a Mozarabic preservation), _moshoseitaro_ (Greek).

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*葫蘆巴 (húlúbā)* is canonical in the Chinese tradition from the *Song dynasty onwards* — appearing in the *Jiayou Bencao* (1057) and extensively in the *Bencao Gangmu*. The Chinese clinical register treats it as a *Kidney-warming, cold-dispelling drug for lower-jiao cold patterns* — the canonical reading for Persian-derived warming aromatic seeds in the Chinese pharmacopoeia.#footnote[葫蘆巴 in _Jiayou Bencao_ (嘉祐本草, 1057) and _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫); some sources mark "hot" (熱) — close to 小茴香 (fennel) and 肉桂 (cinnamon) on the warming axis.

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Bitter (苦 kǔ); some sources include a slight pungent (辛) note.

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Kidney (腎), Liver (肝). The Kidney-and-Liver lower-jiao placement is the canonical reading.

*Actions & indications:*
- *溫腎陽 / 散寒止痛 (wēn shèn yáng / sàn hán zhǐ tòng)* — warms Kidney yang, scatters cold, stops pain. The cardinal use: for *cold-pattern lower-jiao disorders* — cold-pattern hernia (_shàn qì_ 疝氣), scrotal cold-pain, lower abdominal cold-pain, lower back and knee cold-pain.
- *Lower-jiao warming-deobstruent register*: 葫蘆巴 sits alongside 小茴香 (fennel), 補骨脂 (psoralea), 沉香 (agarwood) as the canonical *lower-jiao cold-dispersing* warming-aromatic drugs.
- *Dysmenorrhea from cold pattern* — combines with 艾葉 (mugwort), 肉桂 (cinnamon).
- *External use* — for cold-damp arthritic pain in the lower body, as a warming compress.

*Cautions:* yin-vacuity heat patterns; blazing fire; pregnancy at therapeutic doses (the strong warming-deobstruent action); damp-heat lower-jiao patterns; the bitter-warming intensity is contraindicated in heat-pattern conditions of the lower jiao.#footnote[Actions and cautions: 葫蘆巴 in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; standard modern Chinese pharmacology texts.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

*Methī / methikā* is classical, treated by Caraka in the _śāka-varga_ (leaves) and elsewhere, and by Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu in both the _haritakyādi-varga_ (seed) and the _śāka-varga_ (leaves). The herb has *deep classical, household, and pharmaceutical use* across the Indian subcontinent — both as a seed-drug (the canonical pharmaceutical form) and as a daily green leafy vegetable, particularly in northern Indian and Punjabi cuisines.#footnote[Methī in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_śāka-varga_); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_ (seed) and _Śāka-varga_ (leaf); P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II, _methī_ entry.]

*Rasa (taste):* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter); Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent) — the bitter is the dominant note, with the astringent secondary; the freshly-roasted seed develops a maple-aromatic-sweet undertone (the sotolone register — see modern pharmacology below) but the classical rasa-assignment is firmly bitter-astringent.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light) when properly roasted; Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry); Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp / penetrating); Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous) — when soaked or cooked into porridge, the seed's mucilaginous galactomannan creates a snigdha character. The dry-roasted and the soaked-mucilaginous forms have *different guṇa-readings*, with the classical commentators recognizing this stage-by-stage distinction.

*Vīrya (potency):* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating) — *strongly heating*. The hot vīrya is one of the herb's defining classical features and aligns with the Tibb Hot-Dry mizāj and the Chinese 溫 reading. *Strong cross-system convergence on the warming axis* — unusually clean, given the cross-system divergences common in other entries.

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of methī: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_; the strong uṣṇa vīrya and tikta-kaṣāya rasa anchor the Kapha-vāta-pacifying / Pitta-aggravating doṣa profile.]

*Prabhāva:* The *stanyajanana* (galactagogue) action — strongly developed across all four classical traditions and modern pharmacology — is sometimes read at *prabhāva* level by commentarial sources, since the lactation-promoting effect is not directly predictable from the bitter-astringent-hot-pungent profile. The classical Ayurvedic texts do not explicitly mark it as a prabhāva, however, treating the lactagogue action as derived from the broader _stanyajanana_ category (which includes other bitter-warming drugs); the prabhāva-level reading is interpretive — flagged. The *anti-diabetic / madhumeha-hara* action, also strongly attested classically and confirmed in modern pharmacology, is similarly read at prabhāva level by some modern commentators; again, interpretive — flagged.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies *Kapha and Vāta* (kapha-vāta-śāmaka — the standard bitter-warming-pungent profile); aggravates *Pitta* in excess (the strong uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa).

*Karma (actions):*
- *Dīpana, pācana* — kindles digestion, supports _āma_-digestion. The classical _dīpanī_ name (one of methī's synonyms) reflects this central action.
- *Vāta-anulomana* — relieves Vāta downward (gas, bloating).
- *Stanya-janana / stanya-vardhana* — *galactagogue / lactation-promoting* — the cross-system convergent action. Methī is *the* canonical Indian galactagogue, used in the postpartum (_sūtikā-paricaryā_) regimen across regional traditions.
- *Vṛṣya* — aphrodisiac; reproductive-tissue strengthening.
- *Balya* — strength-giving.
- *Rasāyana* — rejuvenating; methī is included in some rasāyana preparations.
- *Madhumeha-hara* — *anti-diabetic / anti-prameha*; the classical Indian use for diabetes is well attested, particularly in north Indian and Punjabi traditional medicine, and converges precisely with the modern hypoglycemic data.
- *Krimighna* — anthelmintic (the _jantughnī_ name reflects this).
- *Jvarahara* — anti-fever.
- *Hṛdya* — cardiotonic.
- *Tvacya* — skin-benefiting.
- *Keśya* — hair-benefiting (the modern Indian hair-oil preparations with methī seed have classical anchor).
- *Used in:* _stanya-kṣaya_ (low lactation — primary), _madhumeha_ (diabetes — primary), _agnimāndya_ (digestive weakness), _ādhmāna_ (gas), _ārtava-kṣaya_ (oligomenorrhea), _kāsa-śvāsa_ (cough-asthma), _vāta-vyādhi_ (Vāta disorders), _krimi_ (worms), postpartum care (_sūtikā-anna_).

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S) Conditional viruddha:* Pitta excess, _amlapitta_ (hyperacidity), _raktapitta_ (bleeding disorders) — the strong uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa profile is inappropriate. The classical principle applies straightforwardly.
- *(N) Combination cautions:* large doses with sour-and-fermented foods may compound Pitta heat (standard saṃyoga rule); the bitter-warming intensity needs the snigdha vehicle (ghee, milk) in chronic use for Vāta-sensitive constitutions.
- *(F) Folk:* fresh methī leaves with curd / yogurt in heavy quantity is folk-cautioned as digestively heavy.
- *(M) Modern medical:*
  - *Pregnancy* — *uterine stimulant; emmenagogue at high dose; avoid therapeutic doses in pregnancy*. The classical emmenagogue / _garbhāśaya-uttejaka_ register has modern phytopharmacological grounding (the saponin fraction). *Small culinary use is generally considered safe*; therapeutic-dose preparations (concentrated extract, high-dose seed regimens) should be avoided in pregnancy.
  - *Hypoglycemia* — *significant* — the herb has well-documented hypoglycemic activity (see Modern Pharmacology); *combination with antidiabetic medication requires monitoring* — additive hypoglycemic effect is real and clinically relevant. Patients with diabetes using fenugreek should monitor blood glucose.
  - *Anticoagulant interaction* — modest theoretical concern via the coumarin fraction; clinical relevance modest at typical doses.
  - *Hypothyroidism* — modest theoretical interaction with levothyroxine absorption (the fiber binds); separate dosing by 2–4 hours.
  - *Allergy* — *fenugreek-peanut cross-reactivity is well-documented*; both are Fabaceae; patients with peanut allergy may react to fenugreek and should be cautious.
  - *Maple-syrup body-odor* (the *sotolone* effect) — high-dose fenugreek consumption (typical Ayurvedic or Tibb therapeutic doses) produces a *maple-syrup odor in sweat and urine*, due to the sotolone (3-hydroxy-4,5-dimethyl-2(5H)-furanone) component; harmless but striking — *importantly, this may mimic maple-syrup-urine disease (MSUD) in newborns* if the breastfeeding mother is consuming high-dose fenugreek, leading to false-positive MSUD screening. Pediatric considerations apply.
  - *Newborn / infant* — *the sotolone-MSUD false-positive concern is real*; high-dose fenugreek by breastfeeding mothers can confound MSUD screening in newborns.
  - *Androgenic / testosterone-modulating activity* — the diosgenin fraction has modest androgenic-related activity; clinical use as a male-fertility / libido herb has modest support; women with androgen-sensitive conditions should be cautious at therapeutic doses.#footnote[Pregnancy and emmenagogue: classical and modern; the saponin-fraction uterine activity has phytopharmacological grounding. Hypoglycemia: well-documented in multiple RCTs (Sharma 1990; Madar 1988; Sowmya 1999; Kassaian 2009; subsequent reviews). Peanut cross-reactivity: standard allergy literature. Sotolone / MSUD false-positive: documented in pediatric and breastfeeding literature; the sotolone aroma in fenugreek is the same compound that gives maple syrup its characteristic flavor. Verify against current clinical-medicine guidelines.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

*Ḥulba* (حُلْبَة) is *classical* in Tibb — treated by Avicenna in _al-Qānūn_ Book II, Ibn al-Bayṭār in _al-Jāmiʿ_, al-Rāzī in _al-Ḥāwī_, and across the Unani compendia. The herb is a canonical *deobstruent, expectorant, demulcent (paradoxically — the cooked-mucilaginous register), galactagogue, and convalescent-nutritive* drug. There is also a *Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation*, with the famous (though disputed-authenticity) hadith "Were my Ummah to know what is in fenugreek..." discussed below. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the framework.

*Arabic:* حُلْبَة (_ḥulba_) — the canonical Tibb name. Persian: حلبه (_ḥalbah_) and شنبلیله (_shanbalīleh_). Urdu: حلبه (_ḥulbah_) and میتھی (_methī_, Sanskrit borrowing). Maghrebi Arabic: حلبة (_ḥulba_); the seed-and-spice paste *hilbeh* of Yemeni-Jewish and Yemeni-Arab cuisine (eaten with bread on the Sabbath table and as a daily condiment) preserves the Arabic name in culinary register.

*Mizāj:* *Hot in the 2nd degree, Dry in the 1st degree* per Avicenna (_al-Qānūn_ II); some sources read the dryness as 2nd degree. The Tibb classification converges cleanly with the Ayurvedic uṣṇa vīrya and the Chinese 溫 reading — *unusually clean cross-system convergence on the warming axis*. The hot-and-(modestly-)dry placement underpins fenugreek's use in cold-pattern conditions, deobstruent applications, and *paradoxically* its cooked-mucilaginous demulcent use (the seed's heat-warming-and-dispersing action is balanced by the mucilage's surface-protecting demulcent action when cooked into porridge or paste).

*Afʿāl:*
- *Mufattiḥ li-l-suddad* — deobstruent (hepatic, splenic, gastric); a primary action.
- *Muḥallil* — resolvent (for cold-pattern swellings and hardenings).
- *Munaḍḍif li-l-ṣadr* — chest-cleansing (for cough, phlegm, chest tightness).
- *Mudirr li-l-bawl* — diuretic.
- *Mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ* — emmenagogue.
- *Mudirr li-l-laban* — *galactagogue* — the cross-system convergent use; central in Tibb postpartum practice.
- *Muqawwī al-badan / mughadhdhī* — body-strengthening / nutritive.
- *Mulayyin* — mildly laxative (the mucilage register).
- *Muḥarrik al-bāh* — aphrodisiac.
- *Kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* — carminative.
- *Ḍimād* (external poultice) — for skin conditions, sores, ulcers, gangrenous wounds, mastitis; the cooked-paste poultice is a classical Tibb dermatological-and-surgical preparation.
- *Ghidhāʾ ḥasan li-l-naqāhah* — good food for convalescence.

*Istemālāt:* cough; chest congestion; cold-pattern dyspepsia; hepatic and splenic obstruction; constipation (mild); diabetic and prediabetic states (the classical Tibb register on _maraḍ al-ʿaṭash_ / "thirst-disease," a recognized syndrome with overlap to modern diabetes); amenorrhea; low lactation; postpartum recovery; weakness and convalescence; skin ulcers and gangrenous wounds (external); fever (warming-deobstruent use in cold-pattern fever).

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt:* hot-mizāj patients (use moderately); excessive use in pregnancy (uterine-stimulant); chronic high-dose in bilious / hot patterns; epileptic patients (general caution for strong warming-aromatics).

*Muṣliḥ:* *vinegar* (_khall_) — the classical Tibb _muṣliḥ_ for the heating intensity, modifying the hot-dry to a more balanced register; rose-water; honey; ghee or olive oil in cooking — to soften the bitter-astringent edge.

*Badal:* *anīsūn* (anise); *karawiyā* (caraway); *rāziyānaj* (fennel) — the warm-aromatic seed drugs; *zaʿtar* (thyme) for the bronchial register; *seed of nigella* (_ḥabbat al-baraka_, _Nigella sativa_) for the broader prophylactic-nutritive register.

*Classical Tibb formulas:*
- *Ḥasāʾ al-ḥulba* (حساء الحلبة) — *fenugreek-and-honey gruel*; one of the most classical Tibb postpartum and convalescent preparations: seeds cooked into a porridge with honey, dates, milk, and dried fruits. The canonical Tibb postpartum food, paralleling the Ayurvedic _methī laḍḍū_ (see below).
- *Sharbat al-ḥulba* (شربت الحلبة) — fenugreek-honey syrup; for cough, chest congestion, and convalescence.
- *Maʿjūn-i Ḥulba* (معجون الحلبة) — fenugreek electuary in compound Unani preparations; for digestive-weakness, lactation support, postpartum care.
- *Ḥulba in compound aphrodisiac preparations* — the _muḥarrik al-bāh_ register; alongside _zanjabīl_ (ginger), _qarfah_ (cinnamon), _qaranfal_ (clove), and seeds of various warming aromatics in classical compound recipes.
- *External ḍimād preparations* — boiled fenugreek paste as poultice for sores, mastitis, abscesses, gangrenous wounds; one of the standard Tibb dermatological-surgical preparations.
- *Ḥilbeh / hilbeh (Yemeni)* — the Yemeni Arab and Yemeni Jewish *fenugreek paste*: soaked, beaten fenugreek seeds whipped with water, garlic, cilantro, chili, and skoog or _zhug_ paste; eaten with bread daily and at Sabbath/feast tables. Preserved continuously in modern Yemeni and Israeli-Yemeni cuisines.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* The hadith *"law yaʿlamu ummatī mā fī al-ḥulba la-ishtarawhā wa-law bi-waznihā dhahaban"* — *"Were my Ummah to know what is in fenugreek, they would buy it even with its weight in gold"* — is attributed to the Prophet in Tibb al-Nabawī compendia (Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_; al-Suyūṭī, _al-Manhaj al-Sawī_; al-Suyūṭī, _al-Jāmiʿ al-Ṣaghīr_) but is *classified as weak (ḍaʿīf) or fabricated (mawḍūʿ) by the standard hadith-criticism authorities* (al-Bayhaqī notes the chain weakness; modern hadith scholars al-Albānī classify it as ḍaʿīf). The hadith is *widely cited in popular Tibb al-Nabawī literature* and underlies the herb's status in modern Muslim folk medicine, but the *authenticity status should be honestly flagged*. A separate, more reliably attested hadith narrates that the Prophet recommended fenugreek to Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ during his illness ("treat your illness with this fenugreek"), narrated by al-Ṭabarānī and others — though this too has chain-strength caveats. The herb's status in Tibb al-Nabawī is *real but textually qualified* — the cultural attestation in Muslim folk medicine is much stronger than the hadith-critical evidence supports. Honest scholarly register: *Tibb al-Nabawī attestation present but chain-weak; cultural attestation strong; classical Avicennan attestation rock-solid*.#footnote[Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II, _ḥulba_ entry; Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_, _ḥulba_ entry; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_, vol. III, _ḥulba_ entry. Tibb al-Nabawī: Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_; al-Suyūṭī, _al-Manhaj al-Sawī_ and _al-Jāmiʿ al-Ṣaghīr_. Hadith authenticity: the "weight in gold" hadith is in al-Suyūṭī's _al-Jāmiʿ al-Ṣaghīr_ (no. 7373) and discussed in the Tibb al-Nabawī compendia; al-Albānī and contemporary muḥaddithūn classify it as ḍaʿīf or mawḍūʿ. The Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ hadith is in al-Ṭabarānī with chain weakness. The classical Avicennan attestation is independent of the hadith register and is fully solid. Honest scholarly practice flags the hadith authenticity caveat while preserving the substantial cultural and classical-medical attestation.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *葫蘆巴 in compound warming-Kidney formulas* — for cold-pattern hernia (_shàn qì_), lower-jiao cold pain, dysmenorrhea from cold pattern.
- *葫蘆巴丸 (Húlúbā Wán, "Fenugreek Pill")* — classical Song-era formula for cold-pattern hernia.
- *葫蘆巴 + 小茴香 + 烏藥* — the warming lower-jiao trio for cold abdominal pain.
- *External use* — heated fenugreek seed compresses for cold-damp arthritic pain.#footnote[Chinese formulas: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; Song-era warming-Kidney formulas. The clinical center of gravity for 葫蘆巴 is the cold-pattern lower-jiao register, less central than for the Ayurvedic / Tibb medicinal scope.]

=== Ayurveda

- *Methī cūrṇa* — the simple seed powder, 1–3 g with warm water or milk; for digestion, diabetes (modern register), lactation support, mild constipation.
- *Methī laḍḍū / methī pāka* — the *canonical north Indian postpartum sweet*: fenugreek seed + ghee + jaggery + dry fruits + warming spices (cardamom, ginger, ajwain) + edible gum (_gond_). One of the most important traditional postpartum foods of north India and Pakistan, eaten daily for 40 days after delivery for lactation, recovery, and uterine involution. Direct parallel to the Tibb _ḥasāʾ al-ḥulba_ (postpartum gruel).
- *Methī sāg / methī patta* — the *fresh fenugreek leaves* as a green leafy vegetable; classical Punjabi and north Indian household dish (methī aloo, methī paratha, methī chicken, methī dal); the leaves are nutritionally distinct from the seeds (higher in iron, calcium, vitamin C) and dietetically gentler (less heating).
- *Kasūrī methī* — dried fenugreek leaves used as an aromatic in many north Indian dishes; the dry-leaf register has a distinctive maple-aromatic note from the sotolone fraction.
- *Methika cūrṇa, methī ghṛta, methī rasāyana* — various Ayurvedic compound preparations.
- *Methī arka* — distillate, used in some respiratory and digestive applications.
- *Diabetic regimens (modern):* soaked-overnight fenugreek seeds eaten on empty stomach in the morning; the canonical Indian household intervention for diabetic management, with substantive modern clinical support (see below).
- *Methī oil for hair* — traditional oiling preparation; methī seeds in coconut/sesame oil, infused, applied to hair and scalp.#footnote[Ayurvedic formulary: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Haritakyādi-varga_ and _Śāka-varga_; Sharangadhara Saṃhitā; _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_; regional north Indian and Punjabi traditional medicine literature. The methī laḍḍū postpartum tradition is documented across regional north Indian and Pakistani Muslim and Hindu cuisines; the parallel with the Arabic ḥasāʾ al-ḥulba is one of the cleaner cross-tradition convergences in postpartum dietetics.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

- *Greco-Roman: Hippocrates* uses fenugreek (τῆλις, _tēlis_) for women's reproductive and lung conditions. *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ II.124) treats fenugreek extensively: as an emollient poultice for skin sores and abscesses, for women's reproductive conditions (cervical and uterine pessaries, the classical _trochiscus_ register), for cough and bronchial conditions, as a digestive-restorative. *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XXIV) gives extensive uses, including as a wound-poultice and for female reproductive medicine. *Galen* classifies fenugreek as hot in the 2nd degree, drying — converging directly with the later Tibb mizāj.
- *Roman culinary and agricultural use:* Romans imported fenugreek heavily as fodder ("Greek hay" — the Latin name preserves the importation register); also used in cookery (Apicius) as a flavoring.
- *Medieval European pharmacy:* fenugreek persists in Mediterranean monastic and Salernitan pharmacy as an emollient poultice and for women's medicine; the herb travels via the Andalusian-Arab medical synthesis into Latin Western pharmacy (the Spanish _alholva_ name preserves the Arabic _al-ḥulba_).
- *Modern European herbal:* fenugreek seed and seed paste appear in modern European phytotherapy for digestion, diabetes (the most-studied modern use), lactation, and skin applications. *ESCOP* and *Commission E* have monographs on fenugreek for dyspepsia and as a galactagogue; the *EMA* has assessed fenugreek for traditional use.
- *Modern Mediterranean culinary survival:* the Egyptian *helba* (a yellow fenugreek-tea, used as a digestive and for warming), the Greek *moshoseitaro* in compound spice blends, and Levantine *hilbeh* — all preserve continuous Mediterranean culinary register.#footnote[Greco-Roman sources: Hippocrates _passim_; Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ II.124 (_tēlis_) — Beck translation; Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XXIV (Loeb / Rackham); Galen, _On the Properties of Foodstuffs_. Apicius, _De Re Coquinaria_. The Spanish _alholva_ etymology preserves the Arabic transmission to Iberia. ESCOP _Monographs_, _Foenugraeci semen_; Commission E monograph; EMA traditional-use assessment.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above. The Tibb formulary use of ḥulba is *extensively elaborated* — ḥasāʾ al-ḥulba (postpartum gruel), sharbat al-ḥulba (cough syrup), maʿjūn-i ḥulba (digestive electuary), external poultices, and the Yemeni *hilbeh* paste (eaten by both Yemeni Jewish and Yemeni Arab communities at the Sabbath/feast table, a striking shared culinary practice across confessional lines).

=== Jewish (Rosh Hashanah siman)

The Talmudic *rūbya* (רוּבְיָא, Aramaic = fenugreek) is one of the canonical *Rosh Hashanah simanim* (symbolic foods), eaten on the eve of Rosh Hashanah with the prayer *"yehī rāṣōn... she-yirbū zechūyōtēnū"* — *"may it be Your will that our merits increase"* — punning on the Aramaic _rūbya_ (fenugreek) and the Hebrew _r-b-h_ ("to increase, multiply"). The custom is sourced in *Babylonian Talmud, Horayot 12a and Keritot 6a* and is preserved continuously through the medieval and modern Sephardic and Mizrachi-Jewish Rosh Hashanah liturgies. Ashkenazi tradition uses different simanim (apples in honey, pomegranate, etc.). The fenugreek siman is preserved most strongly in Iraqi-Jewish, Yemeni-Jewish, Persian-Jewish, and North-African-Jewish Rosh Hashanah seder customs.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Fenugreek is one of the *oldest cultivated Old-World food-medicines*, with archaeological evidence of cultivation from the Bronze-Age Fertile Crescent (~6000 years ago) — placing it among the earliest of all domesticated herbs. Native to the *eastern Mediterranean / Fertile Crescent* (Iraq, Iran, the Levant), spreading through North Africa, the Mediterranean basin, and South Asia by the Iron Age. Its native climates are *warm-dry Mediterranean* and *hot-arid Middle Eastern* (winter-cropping); modern cultivation extends to *continental seasonal-extreme* (India during the cool winter months — the rabi crop) and *subtropical-humid* (Argentina, parts of China). The dietetic role of the herb tracks each climate, with the *postpartum-warming-galactagogue register* as a striking cross-climate convergence. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Warm-dry Mediterranean / Middle Eastern (the canonical climate)

The Fertile Crescent / Levantine / Mediterranean home climate. *Vāta-Pitta* in hot-dry summer; *Vāta-Kapha* in cool-wet winter; the herb is *winter-cropped* — sown in autumn, harvested in spring — fitting precisely into the climatic agricultural cycle.

*Traditional preparation.* The Levantine and Egyptian use of *hilba / helba* — the hot fenugreek-seed tea, drunk warm with honey — is climatically apt for winter and for cold-pattern dyspepsia in cool weather. Yemeni *hilbeh paste* (with garlic, cilantro, chili, lemon) is a daily warming-aromatic condiment eaten with flatbread. Cypriot and Greek *moshoseitaro* in spice blends. North African *halbah* in tagines and compound spice mixes. The Levantine Jewish *rūbya* dishes at Rosh Hashanah (Iraqi, Yemeni, North African Jewish) are autumn-equinox-timed and seasonally apt for the cooling weather.

*Cares.* The warming-aromatic register is climatically apt for cool-season Mediterranean and Middle Eastern use; the hot-dry-summer use is moderated to compound preparations (with cooling rose-water, sumac, vinegar — the classical Tibb _muṣliḥ_ pairings) and reserved for therapeutic rather than dietary use.

=== South Asian continental / monsoonal (Indian subcontinent — the most-developed culinary register)

The Indian subcontinent has the *most developed* fenugreek culinary tradition globally. *Vāta-Kapha* in cool winter (the rabi growing season); *Pitta-Kapha* in hot monsoon; the herb is the canonical *winter green leafy vegetable* and the canonical *winter warming spice-seed*.

*Traditional preparation.*
- *Methī sāg / methī aloo / methī paratha* — fresh fenugreek leaves as winter vegetable; central in Punjabi and north Indian winter cuisine.
- *Methī laḍḍū / methī pāka* — postpartum warming-galactagogue sweet (covered above).
- *Methī dāl, methī chicken, methī fish* — the leaves as flavoring in compound dishes.
- *Kasūrī methī* — dried leaves as an aromatic in north Indian cuisine year-round; the maple-sotolone note is distinctive.
- *Methī seed in panch phoron* — Bengali five-spice (cumin, fennel, nigella, fenugreek, mustard) — used for dāl and vegetable tempering.
- *Soaked methī seeds for diabetes* — modern Indian household practice; overnight-soaked seeds eaten on empty stomach with the soaking water.
- *Methī chā* (fenugreek tea), *methī rāb* (fenugreek porridge) — Maharashtrian and Gujarati traditional drinks; warming digestive preparations.

*Cares.* The Indian register pairs methī with *snigdha vehicles* (ghee, yogurt, milk) in cool-season and postpartum applications — the classical correction for the bitter-astringent-warming intensity. Hot-summer / monsoon use is moderated; the principal therapeutic and dietary use is in *winter, monsoon-end, and postpartum* (cool-season-equivalent) contexts.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme (Northern China — Song-era Persian introduction)

The Song-era introduction of 葫蘆巴 into Chinese pharmacy fits the *continental seasonal-extreme* climate's cold-dry winter and the Kidney-yang loading from the long northern winter. The Chinese clinical use focuses on *cold-pattern lower-jiao* — climatically apt for the cold-dry winter pole of the continental cycle.

=== Hot-humid tropical (modern global spread)

Modern cultivation in subtropical Argentina, hot-humid southern China, and parts of Southeast Asia represents post-classical extension. In hot-humid Pitta-Kapha climate, fenugreek's strong heating-and-drying register is dietetically heavy — *moderate culinary use in compound preparations* is the climatically apt register; chronic heavy daily use is not classical and is reasonably moderated.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern Western "fenugreek for testosterone" or "fenugreek for breast milk" supplement register has globalized high-dose fenugreek extract use across all climates and constitutional types, often without reference to the herb's hot-dry mizāj or the climatic context. The classical readings — *Mediterranean and Middle Eastern hot-dry-cool-season register*; *Indian rabi-winter-and-postpartum register*; *Chinese cold-pattern lower-jiao register* — all anchor the herb's use to cool-season and constitutionally cool-pattern conditions. High-dose chronic supplement use in hot-summer, in hot-climate constitutions, or in Pitta-loaded patients should be moderated. *The sotolone-maple-urine-MSUD-false-positive concern in breastfeeding mothers* is a modern transposition issue worth flagging explicitly: high-dose galactagogue supplementation by mothers of newborns being screened for MSUD has caused documented false-positive screenings. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the warm-dry and hot-arid climate profiles.#footnote[Climate framework: see `climates.typ`. Mediterranean and Levantine traditions: Roden, _A Book of Middle Eastern Food_; Wolfert, _The Food of Morocco_; the Yemeni hilbeh paste tradition. Indian methī tradition: Achaya, _Indian Food: A Historical Companion_; regional north Indian dietetic literature. The MSUD false-positive concern in breastfeeding fenugreek users is documented in pediatric literature and is a modern globalized-supplement issue.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (dried fenugreek seed, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~323 kcal; Water ~9 g
- Carbohydrate ~58 g; *fiber ~25 g* (very high — half of which is the soluble *galactomannan* mucilage, central to many of the pharmacological actions)
- Protein ~23 g (substantially high for a seed; *4-hydroxyisoleucine* is a distinctive non-protein amino acid)
- Fat ~6 g
- Minerals: iron ~33 mg (very high — relevant to the classical use in convalescence and postpartum), magnesium ~191 mg, calcium ~176 mg, potassium ~770 mg, manganese (high), copper, zinc
- B-vitamins: thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, B6, folate
- *Steroidal saponins:* diosgenin (the principal one — the same compound that is the industrial precursor for many synthetic steroidal pharmaceuticals), trigogenin, gitogenin, yamogenin — the saponin fraction underlies the lactagogue, androgenic-modulating, and some uterine-stimulant effects
- *Alkaloids:* trigonelline (pyridinium alkaloid; bitter; also found in coffee — N-methylnicotinic acid); choline; gentianine
- *Phenolics and flavonoids:* vitexin, isovitexin, orientin, quercetin
- *Furostanol glycosides*
- *4-hydroxyisoleucine* — the distinctive non-protein amino acid responsible for the insulinotropic effect (stimulates pancreatic β-cell insulin secretion)
- *Sotolone (3-hydroxy-4,5-dimethyl-2(5H)-furanone)* — the distinctive maple-aromatic compound; the same compound that gives maple syrup its characteristic aroma; responsible for the "maple-syrup body odor" of high-dose fenugreek consumers and the MSUD-false-positive concern
- *Coumarins:* modest
- *Volatile oil:* small amount, with characteristic celery-like top notes#footnote[Phytochemistry of fenugreek: extensive modern pharmacognosy; standard references (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); ESCOP _Monographs_, _Foenugraeci semen_; recent reviews in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytochemistry_, _Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition_. The 4-hydroxyisoleucine discovery (Sauvaire et al., _Diabetes_ 1998) is one of the more striking modern phytochemical findings.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Hypoglycemic — the headline modern action*. Multiple mechanisms: (a) *4-hydroxyisoleucine* directly stimulates glucose-dependent insulin secretion from pancreatic β-cells; (b) the soluble *galactomannan fiber* slows carbohydrate absorption and blunts postprandial glycemia; (c) the saponin fraction inhibits intestinal sucrose absorption and α-glucosidase. *Multiple RCTs* in type 2 diabetic and prediabetic patients show meaningful reductions in fasting glucose, postprandial glucose, and HbA1c at doses of 5–25 g seed powder per day (Sharma et al. 1990; Madar et al. 1988; Gupta et al. 2001; Kassaian et al. 2009; subsequent meta-analyses). The classical Ayurvedic _madhumeha-hara_ action has clean modern phytopharmacological grounding. *This is one of the better-supported modern herbal hypoglycemic claims.*
- *Cholesterol-lowering* — meta-analyses support reductions in total cholesterol and LDL via the galactomannan fiber (bile-acid sequestration mechanism) and possibly the saponin fraction; ESCOP and Commission E both list this use.
- *Galactagogue / lactation-promoting* — modest clinical evidence; surveys and small RCTs support a lactation-volume increase in breastfeeding mothers. Mechanism unsettled — possibly via the diosgenin / phytoestrogen fraction. The cross-system convergent classical use is well-supported empirically but not yet rigorously characterized mechanistically.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — preclinical, modest clinical.
- *Aphrodisiac / testosterone-modulating* — *modest clinical evidence* for fenugreek extract increasing free testosterone in some male populations; the diosgenin / saponin fraction is the likely mechanism; clinical magnitude is modest, well below pharmaceutical androgens.
- *Wound-healing* — external poultice use (the classical Greco-Roman and Tibb _ḍimād_ register) has preclinical wound-healing support.
- *Anthelmintic* — modest in vitro activity.
- *Sotolone aroma* — the *maple-syrup-urine-and-sweat effect* — harmless cosmetic phenomenon but with the MSUD-false-positive concern in newborns of breastfeeding mothers (covered above).

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Culinary register (leaves and seed in cooking):* unrestricted at normal culinary intake — the seed is used at ~1–2 tsp / 5–10 g per dish in tempering, the leaves as a green vegetable at usual portions.
- *Dietary therapeutic (diabetes management):* *10–25 g of seed powder per day*, typically divided in two doses with meals; soaked-overnight whole seeds at ~5–10 g per day; the standard Indian household intervention. *Clinician supervision warranted for diabetic patients*.
- *Galactagogue use:* 5–10 g of seed powder per day in lactating women; ESCOP-listed.
- *Decoction (Tibb / Western):* 1–3 g per cup, taken 1–3 times daily.
- *Tincture:* 1–3 mL of 1:5 tincture, 2–3 times daily.
- *Concentrated extract (modern supplement):* dose varies widely; 500–1000 mg of standardized extract is typical. *Quality and standardization matter — fenugreek supplement supply is not uniform*.
- *Pregnancy:* *avoid therapeutic doses* — uterine-stimulant and emmenagogue; culinary-register use (a teaspoon of seeds in cooking) is generally considered safe. *Postpartum* use after delivery is the classical register.
- *Diabetes / antidiabetic medication:* *additive hypoglycemic effect is real* — monitor blood glucose; clinician supervision; dose adjustment of pharmaceutical antidiabetic medication may be needed.
- *Hypothyroidism:* separate fenugreek intake from levothyroxine by 2–4 hours (fiber binding).
- *Peanut allergy:* potential cross-reactivity (both Fabaceae); use cautiously.
- *Breastfeeding mothers of newborns being screened for MSUD:* discontinue high-dose fenugreek supplementation around the neonatal-screening window to avoid sotolone false-positive.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.* For pharmacologic-dose use in diabetes, hypothyroidism, pregnancy, lactation, and concurrent medication contexts, clinician supervision is warranted.#footnote[Dose ranges: ESCOP _Monographs_, _Foenugraeci semen_; Commission E; WHO _Monographs on Selected Medicinal Plants_; AYUSH _Ayurvedic Formulary of India_. Clinical hypoglycemic studies: Sharma et al. 1990; Madar et al. 1988; Gupta et al. 2001; Kassaian et al. 2009; meta-analyses by Neelakantan et al. 2014 and Gong et al. 2016. MSUD false-positive: documented pediatric case literature. Verify all clinical figures against current pharmacotherapy, obstetric, and pediatric guidelines.]

